+This change in ranking illustrates a limitation of aggregate forecast metrics. Overall RMSE is dominated by common low amounts, while predictions substantially compress the observed upper tail (Section~\ref{sec:amplitude_diagnostics}). The BU-LSTM-Attention calibration slope, standard-deviation ratio and correlation are consistent with a limited-skill conditional-mean forecast, but do not isolate whether limited predictors, event scarcity, the objective or other model choices dominate the compression. The restricted three-sample timing diagnostic cannot establish an event-wise peak delay because selected samples are not independent storm peaks and some windows overlap. Low CSI at the fixed 1.0~mm\,h$^{-1}$ rule must also be distinguished from ranking ability: AUC remained approximately 0.82--0.87, yet validation-selected cutoffs produced FAR of approximately 0.86--0.92 (Section~\ref{sec:event_detection}). More detections therefore did not translate into reliable high-threshold warnings. \revminor{Similar difficulties in representing high precipitation intensities have been reported in data-driven rainfall forecasting. For example, radar-based deep-learning nowcasts have exhibited spatial smoothing and reduced representation of intense rainfall features \citep{ayzel2020rainnet}. Although the present study concerns regional-mean ERA5 precipitation rather than spatial radar nowcasts, these findings provide a relevant comparison for the observed upper-tail amplitude compression. The underlying causes in our models remain unresolved.}
